.TH HEXDUMP 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
hexdump, hd \- dump files in hexadecimal or a custom format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hexdump
.RB [ \-bcdoxCv ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR format ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR length ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR offset ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B hd
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hexdump
writes the bytes of each
.I file
to standard output in one or more formats.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
The files form a single input: offsets run on from one file to the next,
and
.B \-s
and
.B \-n
apply to the whole.
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped.
.PP
.B hd
is
.B hexdump
with
.B \-C
given before any other option.
.PP
The options
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-e ,
and
.B \-f
each add formats, and may be repeated.
Input is read in blocks, 16 bytes for the built-in formats, and each
block is written in every format in the order given, so
.B \-bc
writes two lines for each 16 bytes.
With none of these options, each line is a 7-digit hexadecimal offset
and eight 2-byte values, each as a space and 4 hexadecimal digits.
.PP
Multibyte values are read little-endian.
The built-in formats write offsets in hexadecimal, widening them past
their minimum width as needed, and after the last line, the offset just
past the end of the input.
In the last block, a value only partly present is completed with zero
bytes, and the line is padded with spaces where the input ran out.
Empty input produces no output.
.PP
Unless
.B \-v
is given, a block identical to the one before it is not written, and
each run of such blocks is replaced by a line holding a single
.BR * .
A partial last block is always written, but when it matches the start of
the block before it, a
.B *
line comes first even if no block was left out.
.SS Format strings
A format string, the argument of
.B \-e
or a line of a
.B \-f
file, is a sequence of units, each of the form
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[\fIiterations\fR][\fB/\fIbytes\fR] \fB"\fIformat\fB"\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
A unit writes
.I format
.I iterations
times, by default once.
Each conversion in
.I format
converts
.I bytes
bytes of input, or without
.IR bytes ,
the default for the conversion.
A byte count must be followed by whitespace, as in
.BR "16/1\ \(dq%02x\(dq" .
.PP
The block size is the most input any one format string converts, and
every format string starts at the beginning of the block.
When the last unit of a format string has no
.I iterations
and converts input, it repeats as often as fits in the block.
On the last of several iterations, a whitespace character at the end of
.I format
is not written, so
.B "4/1\ \(dq%02x\ \(dq"
ends without a space.
In a partial last block, conversions past the end of the input are
written as spaces filling their field width, while the text around them
is still written.
.PP
Text in
.I format
is written as is, after these escape sequences are replaced:
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ee ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \e\e ,
.BI \e ooo
with 1 to 3 octal digits, and
.BI \ex hh
with 1 or 2 hexadecimal digits.
An escape that gives a NUL byte ends the format.
Any other backslash is written as is.
.I format
cannot contain a double quote, but
.B \e042
writes one.
.PP
A conversion is
.B %
followed by optional flags, field width, and precision, as in
.BR printf ,
and one of the following.
Integer conversions also accept the length modifiers
.B l
and
.BR ll ,
but
.B ll
is correct only in an 8-byte
.B %d
or
.BR %i .
Only the first conversion in each quoted format works: the format is
cut off before its second
.BR % ,
but the block size still counts the input of the dropped conversions,
so that much input is never shown.
With a byte count, a format with two conversions other than offsets is
rejected.
Put each conversion in a quoted format of its own.
.TP
.B %c
One byte as a character.
.TP
.BR %d ", " %i
A signed decimal integer of 1, 2, 4, or 8 bytes, with
.B %lld
or
.B %lli
for 8.
Always give a byte count: without one,
.B %d
prints 4 bytes but advances 8, so a later conversion in the same format
string prints garbage, and
.B %lld
counts as no input, so alone it writes nothing.
.TP
.BR %o ", " %u ", " %x ", " %X
An unsigned integer of 1, 2, or 4 bytes, by default 4, in octal,
decimal, or hexadecimal.
.TP
.BR %e ", " %E ", " %f ", " %g ", " %G
A floating-point number of 4 or 8 bytes, by default 8.
.TP
.B %s
A string of
.I bytes
bytes.
Without a byte count, a precision, as in
.BR %.8s ,
is required and gives the length.
The string stops at a NUL byte, and without a precision it runs on past
its bytes to the next NUL byte in memory, so always give one.
.TP
.BR %_ad ", " %_ao ", " %_ax
The offset of the next byte, in decimal, octal, or hexadecimal.
It converts no input.
.TP
.BR %_Ad ", " %_Ao ", " %_Ax
The offset just past the end of the input, written once, after the last
block.
Its unit is written only then, and the units after it in the same format
string never are.
Only the last format string to use it, counting the built-in formats,
is written.
.TP
.B %_c
One byte as
.B \-c
writes it.
.TP
.B %_p
One byte as a character if it is printable ASCII, otherwise a period.
.TP
.B %_u
One byte as a character if it is printable ASCII, a control character as
its lowercase ASCII name, such as
.BR nul ,
.BR lf ,
or
.BR esc ,
0x7f as
.BR del ,
and other bytes in hexadecimal.
.PP
Conversions of one byte accept only a byte count of 1.
.B %%
is rejected, so a format cannot write a literal percent sign.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-bc ,
and an option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-n16 .
Options must come before the file operands, and
.B \-\-
ends them.
.B \-n
and
.B \-s
take a number in decimal, in hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or in octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
optionally followed by a multiplier:
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
for 1024, 1048576, or 1073741824, and
.BR KB ,
.BR MB ,
or
.B GB
for 1000, 1000000, or 1000000000.
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
and
.B G
may be lowercase, and may be followed by
.BR iB ,
as in
.BR MiB .
.TP
.B \-b
One-byte octal: 16 bytes per line, each as a space and 3 octal digits.
.TP
.B \-c
One-byte characters: 16 bytes per line, each right-aligned in 4
columns.
Printable ASCII is written as itself; NUL, BEL, BS, TAB, LF, VT, FF, and
CR as
.BR \e0 ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \ef ,
and
.BR \er ;
and other bytes as 3 octal digits.
.TP
.B \-d
Two-byte unsigned decimal: 8 values per line, each as 3 spaces and 5
digits with leading zeros.
.TP
.B \-o
Two-byte octal: 8 values per line, each as 2 spaces and 6 octal digits.
.TP
.B \-x
Two-byte hexadecimal: 8 values per line, each as 4 spaces and 4
hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.B \-C
Canonical hexadecimal and ASCII: an 8-digit offset, 16 bytes as pairs of
hexadecimal digits in two groups of 8, and the same bytes as text
between
.B |
characters, with a period for each byte that is not printable ASCII.
.TP
.B \-v
Write every block, including repeated ones.
.TP
.BI "\-e " format
Add the format string
.IR format .
.TP
.BI "\-f " file
Add each line of
.I file
as a format string.
Leading whitespace is ignored, and so are empty lines and lines whose
first other character is
.BR # .
CRLF line endings are accepted.
.TP
.BI "\-n " length
Read at most
.I length
bytes of input, from 0 to 2147483647.
.TP
.BI "\-s " offset
Skip
.I offset
bytes from the start of the input.
A file lying wholly within the skip is not read.
The offsets written include the skipped bytes.
When the skip covers all of the input, the built-in formats write only
the final offset.
See
.B NOTES
for limits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B hexdump
reads no environment variables of its own.
For those that affect every busybox applet, see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An input file could not be opened, or there was an invalid option,
number, or format string, an unreadable
.B \-f
file, or a failed seek.
.PP
An input file that cannot be opened is skipped, and the others are still
dumped.
The other errors stop
.B hexdump
at once.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a file in hexadecimal and ASCII:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hd program.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show a file as 2-byte values, whose bytes appear swapped because they
are read little-endian:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hexdump hello.txt
0000000 6548 6c6c 2c6f 7720 726f 646c 0a21
000000e
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show 64 bytes starting at offset 0x200:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hd \-s 0x200 \-n 64 program.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the 4-byte value at offset 0x3c of an EXE, the offset of its PE
header:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hexdump \-s 0x3c \-n 4 \-e \(aq/4 "%x\en"\(aq xxd.exe
80
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print a file as unsigned 2-byte integers, one per line, with decimal
offsets:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hexdump \-v \-e \(aq"%_ad: " /2 "%u\en"\(aq samples.raw
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a file as a list of C hexadecimal constants:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hexdump \-v \-e \(aq/1 "0x%02x, "\(aq key.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print 16 random bytes as 32 hexadecimal digits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ hexdump \-n 16 \-e \(aq16/1 "%02x" "\en"\(aq /dev/urandom
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the util-linux
.BR hexdump :
.IP \(bu 2
Options must come before the file operands.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, and no
.B \-L
or
.BR \-X .
.IP \(bu 2
Each quoted format writes only its first conversion.
.IP \(bu 2
.B %d
and
.B %i
need a byte count.
.PP
.B \-s
seeks with a 32-bit offset.
A skip that ends 2 GiB or more into a file either fails with
.B Invalid argument
or lands at the wrong position, though the offsets written assume the
right one.
On a pipe, the seek does nothing, so the dump starts at the beginning of
the input while its offsets include the skip.
When the bytes left to skip equal the size of a file exactly, that file
is dumped from its start instead of skipped, with wrong offsets.
.PP
Floating-point conversions use the Microsoft C runtime: exponents have
three digits, as in
.BR 1.000000e+000 ,
and infinities and NaNs are written in forms such as
.B 1.#INF00
and
.BR 1.#QNAN0 .
.PP
Input files are read in binary mode, and output lines end with LF, not
CRLF.
The busybox device names, such as
.I /dev/zero
and
.IR /dev/urandom ,
work as input files; see
.BR busybox (1).
A file whose full path reaches
.BR MAX_PATH ,
260 characters, cannot be opened.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR xxd (1)
